<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
        Spring Boot 3.5.16. This version was VERIFIED to exist by listing the
        release tags of the upstream spring-boot repository, not assumed.
        Never bump this to a version you have not checked: a nonexistent
        parent POM fails the build at dependency resolution.

        WHY THIS VERSION, and not 3.4.x with a tomcat.version override:
        Trivy's first scan reported Tomcat CVEs fixed in 10.1.42 and 10.1.44,
        so tomcat.version was pinned to 10.1.44 on Boot 3.4.1. The next scan
        then surfaced FURTHER advisories needing 10.1.45 and 10.1.52.
        Hand pinning a single transitive component means chasing advisories one
        scan at a time, against a parent POM whose other managed versions were
        never tested with that Tomcat.

        The 3.5.x line ships a current Tomcat as part of a coherent, tested
        dependency set. No tomcat.version override is set here on purpose:
        Boot's dependency management supplies the patched version, and pinning
        it back would reintroduce the problem.

        When Trivy reports new framework CVEs, bump THIS version first, and
        verify the target release exists before committing.

        NOTE TO FUTURE EDITORS: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment
        body. Do not paste command lines containing option flags here, and do
        not use hyphen rules as underlines. It makes the POM non parseable.
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.16</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>springboot-gitops-eks</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <name>springboot-gitops-eks</name>
    <description>Spring Boot service delivered to EKS via GitHub Actions, ArgoCD and Argo Rollouts</description>

    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Web layer: serves the landing page and the JSON API -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Actuator: /actuator/health drives k8s probes, /actuator/prometheus feeds canary analysis -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Micrometer Prometheus registry: WITHOUT this, /actuator/prometheus does not exist
             and Argo Rollouts canary analysis has no metrics to query. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <layers>
                        <enabled>true</enabled>
                    </layers>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <!--
        Dependency vulnerability scanning:

        OWASP dependency-check was removed deliberately. Without an NVD API key
        it takes 10 to 40 minutes and exceeded the CI stage timeout.

        Java dependency CVEs are still caught, at a better gate:
          * Trivy scans the BUILT IMAGE in the release stage and fails on
            HIGH/CRITICAL before the image is pushed to ECR. It inspects OS
            packages and application dependencies, including the JARs inside
            the layered Spring Boot image. It caught the Tomcat advisories that
            drove the Boot version above.
          * ECR scan on push re-scans stored images, catching CVEs disclosed
            after the build.

        Keeping this green over time means bumping the Boot version when new
        advisories land, not relaxing the scanner. Enable GitHub Dependabot
        (free on public repositories) to get those bumps proposed automatically.
    -->
</project>
